    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L03-ACTIVITY-012",
      "SourceSection": "3.4: Around a Bike Wheel",
      "SourceExcerpt": "What is the circumference of this wheel?",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l03.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L03-ACTIVITY-013",
      "SourceSection": "3.4: Around a Bike Wheel",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Mark the point \\(Q\\) where \\(P\\) will be after the wheel has rolled 1 foot to the left. Be prepared to explain your reasoning.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l03.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L03-ACTIVITY-014",
      "SourceSection": "3.4: Around a Bike Wheel",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Mark the point \\(R\\) where \\(P\\) will be after the wheel has rolled 3 feet to the left. What angle, in radians, does \\(P\\) rotate through to get to \\(R\\)? Explain your reasoning.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l03.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L03-ACTIVITY-015",
      "SourceSection": "3.4: Around a Bike Wheel",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Where will point \\(P\\) be after the bike has traveled \\(\\pi\\) feet to the left? What about \\(10 \\pi\\) feet? \\(100 \\pi\\) feet? Mark these points on the circle. Explain your reasoning.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l03.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L03-ACTIVITY-016",
      "SourceSection": "3.4: Around a Bike Wheel",
      "SourceExcerpt": "After traveling some distance to the left, the point \\(P\\) is at the lowest location in its rotation. How far might the bike have traveled? Explain your reasoning.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l03.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L03-ACTIVITY-017",
      "SourceSection": "3.4: Around a Bike Wheel",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Picture the bicycle with a bright light at point \\(P\\) and moving now from left to right. As the bike passes in front of you going left to right, what shape do you think the light would trace in the air?",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l03.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L03-ACTIVITY-018",
      "SourceSection": "3.4: Around a Bike Wheel",
      "SourceExcerpt": "One way to define a circle centered at \\((0,0)\\) is by the equation \\(x^2+y^2=r^2\\), where \\(r\\) is the radius of the circle. A unit circle has \\(r=1\\), so the equation for this unit circle with center \\(O\\) must be \\(x^2+y^2=1\\). Points…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l03.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L03-ACTIVITY-019",
      "SourceSection": "3.4: Around a Bike Wheel",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Radians are a natural tool to use to measure the distance traveled on a circle. Let’s say that the wheels on a bike have a radius of 1 foot. When the bike starts to move to the left, rotating the wheel counterclockwise, let’s think about…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l03.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L03-ACTIVITY-020",
      "SourceSection": "3.4: Around a Bike Wheel",
      "SourceExcerpt": "The point \\(P\\) will return to its starting location when the wheel has rotated through an angle of \\(2\\pi\\) radians. During this rotation, the bike will move a length equal to the circumference of the wheel, which is \\(2\\pi\\) feet. In…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l03.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L03-ACTIVITY-021",
      "SourceSection": "3.4: Around a Bike Wheel",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Thinking about the wheel as a unit circle, as shown in this image, the arc length of the circle from \\(P\\) to \\(Q\\) has length equal to 1 unit, the radius of the unit circle. Because of this, the angle \\(POQ\\) is said to measure one…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l03.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L03-ACTIVITY-022",
      "SourceSection": "3.4: Around a Bike Wheel",
      "SourceExcerpt": "This makes sense because the ratio of the circumference to the diameter for a circle is \\(\\pi\\), and so the circumference is \\(2\\pi\\) times the radius, or about 6.3 radii.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l03.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L03-ACTIVITY-023",
      "SourceSection": "3.4: Around a Bike Wheel",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Let’s think about some other angles on the unit circle. Here, angle \\(POR\\) measures \\(\\pi\\) radians because its arc is \\(\\frac{1}{2}\\) of a full circle (counterclockwise) or \\(\\frac{1}{2}\\) of \\(2\\pi\\). Angle \\(POU\\) is three quarters of…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l03.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L03-ACTIVITY-024",
      "SourceSection": "3.4: Around a Bike Wheel",
      "SourceExcerpt": "The length of an interval at which a periodic function repeats. A function \\(f\\) has a period, \\(p\\), if \\(f(x+p) = f(x)\\) for all inputs \\(x\\).",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l03.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L03-ACTIVITY-025",
      "SourceSection": "3.4: Around a Bike Wheel",
      "SourceExcerpt": "A function whose values repeat at regular intervals. If \\(f\\) is a periodic function then there is a number \\(p\\), called the period, so that \\(f(x + p) = f(x)\\) for all inputs \\(x\\).",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l03.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L03-ACTIVITY-026",
      "SourceSection": "3.4: Around a Bike Wheel",
      "SourceExcerpt": "The circle in the coordinate plane with radius 1 and center the origin.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l03.rich-lesson-v2.json",
